                "title": "Operation and Maintenance of Hebburn and VENJ Heat Networks",
                "status": "complete",
                "classification": {
                    "scheme": "CPV",
                    "id": "50720000",
                    "description": "Repair and maintenance services of central heating"
                },
                "mainProcurementCategory": "services",
                "description": "South Tyneside Council is appointing an operation & maintenance contractor for two heat networks in Jarrow (VENJ) and Hebburn Renewable Energy Network that were completed in September 2023.",
                "lots": [
                    {
                        "id": "1",
                        "description": "VENJ\nThe VENJ network currently feeds 9 Council buildings (3 with 44 flats each) with heat via buried pipework and 5 with private wire electricity via HV link. The energy centre comprises a 700kWth ammonia river source heat pump with river abstraction filters and pumps, 2 no. 850kW Hoval gas boilers, 43kWe CHP, 4 heating pumps, associated DH water treatment, 100m3 thermal storage & 90 no. (225m2) solar thermal system. System temperatures are 75/55 by design and 73/58 in practice. Hydraulic separation to buildings is provided by Danfoss heat exchange skids with ECL310 controllers & Kamstrup 603 heat meters networked back to the energy centre BMS over fibre. Electrical equipment includes HV switchgear, 2MVA HV/LV transformer, 1MW Solar PV farm and a containerised 500kWh, 200kW LiOn battery. There is scope to expand heat supply to 20 buildings in future phases. The energy centre is an industrial profiled steel building on a brownfield site off Blackett St. Current annual heat load is approx. 2.6MWh p.a.\nHebburn Renewable Energy Network\nHebburn Renewable Energy Network supplies heat via buried pipework to Durham Court (111 flats), Hebburn Central leisure centre, a children's home and other buildings in development.  The energy centre is an architectural  design adjacent to Mountbatten shopping centre. Main heat source is a 500kW cascade heat pump forming primary heat supply at 75/65'C drawing from fan coil units on the roof. Secondary heat is provided by 3 no. Hoval boilers located in the adjacent leisure centre, Hebburn Central, and 125kWe CHP. There are two sets of DH pumps and associated water treatment and pressurisation units. Hydraulic separation from Durham Court is plate heat exchangers, effectively the network primary is part of Hebburn Central LTHW system. Metering is by Kamstrup 603 series. LV electricity is drawn from Hebburn Central via private wire. Annual heat demand is approx. 2MWh p.a.",
                        "awardCriteria": {
